A||||12||loafer87gt||Looking for ideas. Maybe you folks can help.||||3||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||11-19-2001||11:12 PM||||I am working on a sculpture project for my final assingment in one of my art classes. The materials I chose to use are a whole whack of old computer components that I saved from going to the trash. My idea behind the project is how fast things change technology wise; for example, these computer parts would have been groundbreaking only ten years ago, and say, for example, if they ended up in the hands of the Nazi's during the second WW they could have changed the history all together. But at present they are outdated and for the most part useless. I am trying to think of other items that were extremely innovative when they first came out giving those who used them a technological edge over others without them, but through time became obsolete and were eventually replaced by newer, improved versions. Just thought I'd see what ideas you guys come up with. <p>[This message has been edited by loafer87gt (edited 11-19-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000001||Formula88||11-19-2001||11:46 PM||||Maybe some research into mechanical controls?  With the advent of electronic controls, mechanical ones have become, largely, obsolete.  i.e. The mechanical fuel injection system on a mid-60's Corvette was ground breaking in it's time.<P>Design has changed due to modern convenience.  Old houses were built by design to create natural drafts for cooling, etc.  New houses give no thought to air flow through the house because they used forced air heating and cooling.  Only the "air change", or the frequency with which fresh air changes out with the stale house air, becomes an issue.  Some new houses are so airtight, they even have to design in a way to bring in outside air to keep the air from getting stale.<P>Glow in the dark stuff - used only for novelty now, used to be high technology.  Everything now has it's own lightsource, either Indiglo, or LED, or backlit LCD, or something similar.  About the only glow in the dark technology that has advanced is used on night sights for handguns.<P>Self winding watches.  They used to be the rage - your movement kept the watch wound.  With the advent of batteries and solar power, they became unnecessary technology.  Seiko has only recently reintroduced this "new" technology in it's Kinetic line.  These watches were common place in the 60's, but the technology is being advertised and ground-breaking today, simply because it's been out of circulation for so long.<P>Mechanical calcuators.  Remember adding machines?  I remember the ones that used a big lever to register the mechanical inputs.  Then electrical ones came out, but were basically an electrically run lever, instead of you manually pulling it.  The IC chip made those disappear pretty fast.  They would have been powerful calcualtion tools in the WWII era world.  I remember playing with one my dad had as a child back in the '70's.  I tried to get it to divide by 0 - and it started calculating and wouldn't stop!  Had to unplug it to get it to stop.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||stimpy||11-19-2001||11:53 PM||||Howzabout a terminal with the tube removed and little cave dwelling Neanderthals living inside?||||reg||1
