Z||000008||Ken Wittlief||11-20-2001||11:56 AM||||I got a box full of laser tubes and power supplies at an estate auction for $1<P> I think at least 4 of them work - they look really cool with the covers off whenthey are on.<P> they originally sold for $400 or more each<P> but with the advnet of laser diodes ,they are obsolete and useless<P> in fact, I know someone who has one of the original laser disks players - he opened it up and it has a real laser tube in it!<P> I never knew that.<P> One industry that was racked by obsolence was the stream engine railroad system. They started out buring wood, and went on to coal, and then oil - but things progressed so fast that as soon as a company had a new steam engine onthe tracks it was already beign replaced by something bigger and faster.<P>  Also 8mm movie cameras and projects - they were a major ticket item for families from the '50s to the '70s, then they were replaced by camcorders.<P> snapshot film cameras are already well on the way out.||||reg||10
Z||000009||Raydar||11-20-2001||12:02 PM||||From vacuum tubes to transistors to ICs to CPUs. All based on the concept of ones and zeros. Or "on" and "off". Illustrate the difference in voltage requirements/current draw.<P>To put your computer thing in perspective, the guidance system that landed the Lunar Lander on the moon was the functional equivalent of a 486.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> No telling how many rooms it took up.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
